Widescreen, side-on paths: a straight edge where the level has no geometry.

Reported after 0053: the black triangles at the edges of 2D scenes are still
there. Offline with the game's camera, what remains is void: in the extra
columns there is no geometry in any world of the zone or its neighbours
(1.3% of Snow Go's side-on extra columns, up to a fifth in a few views), so
the cleared frame shows between the polygon edges where the modelled scene
ends - wedges. Nothing can be drawn there.

crash2_wide_slst.h, side-on paths only (forward paths unchanged):
- each frame, the polygons the world draw gets are rasterised as the renderer
  will project them (its screen test, the GPU's size limit) at 2-px cells
  over the extra columns and a 16-px strip of the 4:3 frame, each triangle
  grown 1.5 px so cracks between polygons do not count;
- void reaching the strip is open space the 4:3 view shows too, and gaps
  inside the extra columns are as dark as the 4:3 view's own: both stay;
- a side whose 48 px of the 4:3 frame show void of their own (over 2%) is a
  dark, open scene (S000000F's caves), where more darkness at the edge is no
  wedge: no cover there;
- the rest of the void reaching the frame's outer edge is covered, from that
  edge to its innermost cell, by one black opaque POLY_F4 per side linked
  into the world's ordering-table slot 2046, after all the scenery; no draw
  mode is touched;
- the cover grows at once, holds 15 frames once less is needed, then shrinks
  2 px a frame; it never enters the 4:3 frame.

Counters: wide_slst cover_l / cover_r (px) and covered (frames);
crash2_wide_slst_stats fills 17 values.

tuning/wide_frustum_sim: frustum_ref.py scans the same way in float32 and the
C matches it exactly on all 72 side-on cases (33 with a cover), at -O0
to -O3. Later frames 0.18 ms on average, 0.65 ms at most.

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake.

